; A CBOR tag wrapping a homogeneous table (`#6.n({ * k => v })`). The rule FORCE-WRAPS into a
; newtype struct that owns ALL of its serialization including the tag: serialize writes
; `write_tag(11)` then the map, deserialize rejects a wrong tag with
; `DeserializeFailure::TagMismatch`, and `TaggedTable::to_cbor_bytes()` therefore writes the same
; bytes an embed site does. A transparent `pub type TaggedTable = BTreeMap<String, u64>;` could not:
; the tag rode the alias entry, so every embed site wrote it while the rule's own standalone codec
; was `BTreeMap`'s and dropped it — one CDDL type with two wire forms, which is what T1-13 closed.
; Under `--wasm=true` the emitted wasm
; wrapper is accessors-only and DELEGATES serialization to that rust type, so it carries no tag logic
; of its own — pinning that the wasm wrapper generates and compiles for a tagged table (regression:
; `codegen_table_type` used to `assert!(tag.is_none())` and panic on exactly this shape).
; The value type is `uint` (not `int`) on purpose: an `int`-valued table would drag in the separate,
; still-unresolved undefined-`Int` extern class, so `uint` keeps this fixture pinning ONLY the tag case.
; `holder` embeds it so the member/embed role compiles alongside the standalone rule.
tagged_table = #6.11({ * tstr => uint })
holder = [payload: tagged_table]
